  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM EDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis: North American pattern anchored by impressive \n600dam 500mb high (+3 sigma height anomaly) encompassing much of the \nsouthern U.S.  Split flow across the western U.S. between strong \nzonal flow across western Canada and a stalling upper low over \nCalifornia; Great Lakes under the influence of the southern branch \nof this split with a strong jest streak across northern Ontario/ \nQuebec.  Low amplitude short wave crossing the upper Lakes \ntoday...part of a long (latent) PV filament that stretches from \nQuebec all the way back to the California upper low.  West-east axis \nof deeper moisture rotating around the northern periphery of the \nsouthern U.S. anticyclone...from the mid Mississippi Valley east \nacross the Ohio Valley and into the Mid Atlantic/southern New \nEngland.  Also along the northern periphery the upper high stretches \na surface front from New England back into the central Plains... \nserving as an unrelenting corridor of rainfall south and west of \nMichigan for the most part.  Broad high pressure extends from \ncentral Canada across the upper Lakes and into Quebec.\n\nPrimary Forecast Concerns: \n\nRain chances return for the weekend...but probabilities/evolution \nare in question (some areas may not see precipitation at all):\nLooks like we will be able to squeeze out a dry day Friday; high\npressure over Manitoba and ridging into the upper Midwest/Great\nLakes this afternoon will propagate into northern Ontario\nFriday...increasing northeast boundary layer flow into northern\nMichigan. Mid/high clouds will thicken during the afternoon...\nprecipitation shield across Minnesota/Wisconsin driven by\nisentropic ascent and entrance region jet forcing will spread\nacross Lake Michigan by early Friday evening. But with\nconvection likely developing across Iowa Friday afternoon/ \nevening where the low level jet intersects the warm frontal \nboundary...this may tend to allow the precip shield to propagate\nmore southeast with time. The implication is that precipitation\nmay be confined to about the southwest half of the forecast \narea Friday night/Saturday. This is definitely a trend to the \nsouth with this forecast...probabilities of exceeding 1 inch/24 \nhours through 0000Z Sunday around 40% across Manistee county and\ndrops below 10% north of M-32. In fact current forecast will \nnot carry PoPs north of a Charlevoix-Harrisville line through \nthe weekend. Rain chances will linger south of M-32 into Sunday \nthough anticipating a decreasing trend from north to south. \nOverall highest rainfall amounts Friday night through Sunday \nafternoon 0.50- 1.00 inch possible along-south of M-55. \n\nSeasonably cool Monday through midweek: High pressure to the north\nof the Great Lakes through midweek should result in a preponderance\nof east/northeast boundary layer flow into Michigan through \nWednesday. This will keep temperatures generally in the 60s from\nMonday through the middle of the week...which will be a few \ndegrees below normal though we are beginning our foray into \nnormal daytime highs in the 60s. Remnants of the upper low over \nCalifornia could bring some rain chances back to the region by \nmidweek.",
